{"title":"Fillhaus Refill","sub":"Whose footfall forecast do we believe?","type":"Investment / capacity","industry":"Sustainable retail","geo":"Australia","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Break-even · forecast risk · negotiation","prompt":"Fillhaus runs two zero-waste refill shops in Melbourne, where customers bring their own containers for dry food, oils and cleaning products. A shopping-centre landlord has offered a site in a new development and says it will bring about 90 customers a day. The fit-out costs $160k. Should Fillhaus open?","clarify":[{"q":"What do Fillhaus's existing shops do?","a":"Around 55 customers a day each, after two years of trading."},{"q":"Basket and margin?","a":"Average basket $24; gross margin about 45%."},{"q":"Costs?","a":"Rent $60k, two staff $130k, other $20k a year."},{"q":"Trading days?","a":"About 310 a year."},{"q":"Hurdle?","a":"Payback within three years."}],"structure":{"root":"Should Fillhaus open the new shop?","branches":[{"head":"Customers per day","items":["Landlord's forecast","Fillhaus's own shops as a benchmark","New development still filling up?"]},{"head":"Spend per customer","items":["Basket size","Gross margin"]},{"head":"Costs","items":["Rent, staff, other","Fit-out"]}]},"hypothesis":"The landlord has an incentive to be optimistic. I'd test the decision against Fillhaus's own shops: if it only works at 90 customers a day, it's risky.","questions":[{"q":"Calculate annual profit using the landlord's forecast and using Fillhaus's own shops as a benchmark.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"393.86pt\" height=\"176.64pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 393.86 176.64\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 176.64 L 393.86 176.64 L 393.86 0 L 0 0 L 0 176.64 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"